%I A399358 #15 Nov 12 2026 21:32:55
%S A399358 13,43,61,67,79,97,127,163,211,349,397,433,547,577,733,823,907,967,
%T A399358 991,1051,1237,1249,1303,1669,1831,2089,2143,2251,2281,2503,2647,2677,
%U A399358 2731,3271,3343,3511,3571,3673,3691,3739,3853,3931,4159,4243,4447,4513,4549
%N A399358 Primes p > 3 such that the elliptic curve y^2 = x^3 + 7 over GF(p) has a prime number of points (including the point at infinity).
%C A399358 The curve y^2 = x^3 + 7 is the curve secp256k1 used in Bitcoin; for p = 2^256 - 2^32 - 977 it has prime order (cofactor 1), so this p is a (very large) term.
%C A399358 No prime p == 2 (mod 3) is a term (the curve is supersingular with p+1 points, an even number). 7 is not a term (the curve is singular mod 7).
%C A399358 Over GF(p), p == 1 (mod 3), p = c^2 - c*d + d^2, the number of points of y^2 = x^3 + b takes six possible values p + 1 - {+-(2c-d), +-(c+d), +-(c-2d)}, depending on b modulo sixth powers (see the link to Wu and Xu).
%C A399358 Every term p satisfies p == 1 (mod 3), 7 is a quadratic nonresidue mod p (otherwise (0, sqrt(7)) has order 3), and -7 is a cubic nonresidue mod p (otherwise there is a point of order 2). Hence p == 13, 43, 61, 67, 73 or 79 (mod 84).
%C A399358 The number of points N is == 1 (mod 6) for every term, since N = Norm(1-pi) with pi in Z[w] the Frobenius element and x^2 - x*y + y^2 is only 0 or 1 (mod 3); hence x^2+x+1 has a root mod N, which gives the endomorphism used in the GLV method.
%C A399358 For a prime q == 2 (mod 3), q divides N only if p == 1 (mod q).
%C A399358 Among primes p == 1 (mod 3), N is divisible by 7 with density 1/6 if p == 1 (mod 7) and 1/3 otherwise (by cubic and quadratic reciprocity pi mod 84 determines N mod 84, and primary primes of Z[w] are equidistributed among the 432 admissible classes mod 84). Hence terms are more frequent for p == 43 (mod 84): 1160 of the 5670 terms below 2*10^6, versus 863 to 933 in each of the other five classes.
%C A399358 Heuristically (Koblitz-Zywina conjecture), the sequence is infinite, with about C*x/log(x)^2 terms <= x, C = 0.505...; there are 5670 terms below 2*10^6 versus about 5665 predicted. Infinitude is not known.
%D A399358 Kenneth Ireland and Michael Rosen, A Classical Introduction to Modern Number Theory, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 84, Springer-Verlag, 1990, Chapter 18, Section 3. ISBN 978-0-387-97329-6.
%H A399358 Sajid Khan Hussain, <a href="/A399358/b399358.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..5670</a>
%H A399358 Bitcoin Wiki, <a href="https://en.bitcoin.it/wiki/Secp256k1">Secp256k1</a>.
%H A399358 Certicom Research, <a href="https://www.secg.org/sec2-v2.pdf">SEC 2: Recommended Elliptic Curve Domain Parameters</a>, Version 2.0, January 27, 2010.
%H A399358 Han Wu and Guangwu Xu, <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1136">A Note on Koblitz Curves over Prime Fields</a>, Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2020/1136, 2020.
%H A399358 David Zywina, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.5280">A refinement of Koblitz's conjecture</a>, arXiv:0909.5280 [math.NT], 2009.
%F A399358 Let p == 1 (mod 3), p > 7, and p = pi*conj(pi) in Z[w] (w a primitive cube root of unity) with pi primary (pi == 2 mod 3). Then the number of points is N = p + 1 + conj(z)*pi + z*conj(pi) with z = (28/pi)_6 (sextic residue symbol), and p is a term iff N is prime. See Ireland and Rosen, Chapter 18, Section 3.
%e A399358 a(1) = 13: over GF(13) the curve has 7 points, which is prime.
%e A399358 a(2) = 43: over GF(43) the curve has 31 points, which is prime.
%o A399358 (PARI) forprime(p=5, 5000, if(p!=7 && isprime(ellcard(ellinit([0,7], p))), print1(p", ")))
%o A399358 (Python)
%o A399358 from sympy import primerange, isprime
%o A399358 def N(p):
%o A399358     e = (p-1)//2
%o A399358     return 1 + sum(1 if (r:=(x**3+7)%p)==0 else (2 if pow(r,e,p)==1 else 0) for x in range(p))
%o A399358 print([p for p in primerange(5, 5000) if p != 7 and isprime(N(p))])
%Y A399358 Cf. A000040.
